  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWater vapor/RAP analysis was indicating shortwave ridging across the \ncentral CONUS while yesterday's shortwave trough was lifting across \nthe Great Lakes area and another robust mid/upper trough was \nimpacting the Pacific Northwest/California. The sfc pressure \ngradient is progged to ramp up once again tonight into Friday \nbringing increasing southerly winds to the area. Time/height X-\nsections of wind & mixing layer height support peak gusts around 46 \nknots or 53 mph. A wind advisory will likely be needed as we move \ninto the day on Friday. Mild, above average temperatures are \nanticipated Fri-Sat ahead of this incoming storm system with highs \nin the upper 60s/lower 70s for most areas.\n\nA southern stream shortwave trough is progged to sweep across the \nSouthern Rockies emerging over the Central Plains late Saturday \nbringing increasing probabilities (60-90%) for showers with some \nembedded thunderstorms during the evening hours as a cold front \nmoves across the area. A line of storms appears to be the most \nlikely storm mode with some strong wind gusts possible, although \nMUCAPE around 500 J/KG and some modest mid-lvl lapse rates could \nresult in some pockets of small hail.\n\nA stable post-frontal regime will build over the area on Sunday-\nWednesday with cooler more seasonable air expected to settle over \nthe area. We continue to watch a second shortwave trough emerge from \nthe Rockies towards the middle of the week, but this progressive \nsystem is expected to only result in light precipitation with \nhigher probabilities for a light wintry mix remaining further \nwest across parts of western Kansas early on Tue. Shortwave \nridging is progged to build over the area towards the latter \nportion of the week with below average temperatures persisting \nacross the Central Plains. We continue to see some amplitude \ndifferences with a Northern Plains shortwave trough late in the \nweek leading to low confidence in precipitation chances at this \ntime.",
